A||||6||Buster||correct radiator cap question||||1||
Z||000000||Buster||10-05-2000||10:55 PM||||I have replaced all hoses after a drainand flush. I bought a new housing cap and a new radiator cap. I filled from the housing till the radiator was full, then put the cap on and continued to fill. Result was that coolant came out the overflow. The difference in the old cap and the new is that the small metal disk that allows coolant to return from the overflow tank was lightly spring loaded in the closed position while the new one opens via gravity and allows the additionall fluid to freely escape to the bottle. Since the hiegt of the entire volume of coolant is above this disk valve area I can understand that on this system it has to be spring loaded. None of my available auto parts can supply this. Can some one give me a lead on obtaining correct cap,or explain what I may be overlooking. Thanks    Buster||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||10-05-2000||11:14 PM||||I had the same problem. You bought a Stant cap didn't you. That damn Stant catalog is full of $%^&.<P>You need 16lb release <B>non</B>-vented. You bought vented, as the catalog sugested.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||10-05-2000||11:19 PM||||Aha!  Someone else has noticed this phenomenon!<P>I recently showed up at the local discount auto parts store, and asked them for a new cap.  They asked "Why? Your hat looks just fine  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!"<BR>J/K - They didn't say that - only Stu would be <B>that</B> corny. <P>Anyway, they brought out a radiator cap.  I noticed that its little sealing disc flopped open on its own.  I told them I didn't think that looked right - so I made them go back and find me another one.<P>Think the little disc is <B>supposed</B> to be lightly sprung closed.  Pressure from within seals it tighter, and when the car cools down, it sucks the little disc open to draw in coolant from the overflow bottle.  So goes my theory, anyway.  ||||reg||10
Z||000003||ROB MAC||10-05-2000||11:28 PM||||When it comes to rad cap, thermostat, belts and hoses- G.M. DEALER  <BR>Rad caps with valves, fail safe thermostats, grooved and ribbed belts , and universal hoses.....CRAP!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||WisconsinGT||10-08-2000||01:24 PM||||I found out that I have the wrong Stant rad. cap on my 88GT. So I go to the local Farm&Fleet to find out there are two 16 pound caps with the lock thing on the top. Would someone know the part # I should be looking for? Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Buster||10-08-2000||09:42 PM||||Well I may have found an answer. The cap recommended by Stand was an 11231. In looking at the rack their # 11230 appeared<BR>the same except that it had the belived required light spring closure on the return flow valve disk. In searching their book iseems many GM products made this switch from 1977 going to 1978. The next day with calipers in hand and the original cap I went to CarQuest and asked for cap for a 1977 C10 pickup with a 350 V8. It mesured correctly and I have ran my Formula 3 times now andall seems fine. I can not believe that I am on my third top off of the thermostat housing, but It may be the last. For information the Roney Dickman ,low temp fan switch works nice, altho my gage shows 220 when it kicks on. I think it is off some.   Thanks for the support and I hope this helps.  Buster||||reg||1
Z||000006||Brian84SE||10-09-2000||12:26 PM||||I had the same problem too when trying to burp the system(with my new cap).  Fiero56 pointed out that I need the non-vented type. Got one and everything has been "cool" ever since.....||||reg||1
